31:08.000 --> 31:14.000
Good morning. I have what I think is a treat for you this morning. 

31:14.000 --> 31:17.000
You’re going to hear the skirl of the pipes.

31:17.000 --> 31:22.000
But then I have Scotch blood in my veins. Maybe that’s why I think it’s special.

31:22.000 --> 31:27.000
I have the Gaelic College, Sydney, Girls’ Pipers’ Band right here,

31:27.000 --> 31:32.000
and they have their pipes and drums with them, ready to burst into music any moment,

31:32.000 --> 31:40.000
and their director the Rev. A.W.R. MacKenzie is here too, and their instructor Danny MacIntyre.

31:40.000 --> 31:46.000
So first we’ll have a little taste of their music, and I’ll tell you more about these girl pipers later.

31:46.000 --> 31:49.000
Now this is their theme song “Bonnie Dundee”.

32:36.000 --> 32:41.000
Now I’m going to ask the Rev. Mr MacKenzie, their director, about this band.

32:41.000 --> 32:46.000
He has the School of Piping at the Gaelic College at St Anne’s.

32:46.000 --> 32:52.000
You’re the director and organizer of the Gaelic College at St Anne’s aren’t you, Mr MacKenzie?

32:52.000 --> 32:57.000
Yes. I have been director of the Gaelic College and the Gaelic Mòd for fifteen years.

32:57.000 --> 33:01.000
You teach other things besides piping at your college don’t you?

33:01.000 --> 33:09.000
Yes. We have a School of Gaelic, School of Highland Folk Art, a
School of Clan Lore, and a School of Handcrafts.

33:09.000 --> 33:11.000
Now, how many piping instructors do you have?

33:11.000 --> 33:16.000
We have five piping instructors and three drumming instructors.

33:16.000 --> 33:19.000
And how many students would you have there?

33:19.000 --> 33:23.000
At our summer school at St Anne’s this summer, we had one hundred and twelve, 

33:23.000 --> 33:28.000
and at our winter school in Sydney two hundred and sixty six students.

33:28.000 --> 33:29.000
Well, that is a large number of students.

33:29.000 --> 33:33.000
We’ll be having a great number of pipers who were there at times.

33:33.000 --> 33:35.000
And now about these girls. 

33:35.000 --> 33:39.000
They’re all here in front of me, and a bonnie lot they are.

33:39.000 --> 33:44.000
There are twenty-two in the band, all in Highland costume,

33:44.000 --> 33:49.000
and they range in age from about eight or nine to sixteen years old.

33:49.000 --> 33:54.000
And they have velvet jackets that are called doublets,

33:54.000 --> 33:57.000
and tartan skirts are the kilts. 

33:57.000 --> 34:01.000
And each has a sporran attached to the belt,

34:01.000 --> 34:10.000
and they have white frilly lace jabots or lace fronts under those jackets or doublets,

34:10.000 --> 34:13.000
and they have white lace ruffles at their wrists.

34:13.000 --> 34:16.000
And their hats, I mustn’t forget them.

34:16.000 --> 34:19.000
They’re velvet berets, and they have a feather in the front,

34:19.000 --> 34:22.000
or two feathers, a white and a black feather,

34:22.000 --> 34:26.000
and the hats are called Balmorals.

34:26.000 --> 34:31.000
And now, they’re going to play “The 43rd Highlanders” for you.

35:57.000 --> 36:01.000
This band, I notice, has four kinds of instruments – 

36:01.000 --> 36:04.000
the pipes, the big bass drum,

36:04.000 --> 36:07.000
and the side drum and the tenor drum.

36:07.000 --> 36:11.000
So now, I thought you might like to hear how they sound separately. 

36:11.000 --> 36:17.000
Now first, one of the pipers will play a few bars on the pipes. 

36:17.000 --> 36:24.000
So here are the pipes alone.

36:34.000 --> 36:39.000
Now here are the pipes with the side drum added.

36:52.000 --> 36:55.000
Did you hear that drum? 

36:55.000 --> 36:58.000
Now here are the pipes with the tenor drum.

37:14.000 --> 37:17.000
I’m sure you heard that tenor drum. 

37:17.000 --> 37:21.000
Now here are the pipes with all three drums.

37:40.000 --> 37:47.000
Now this band, besides having a director and instructor, 

37:47.000 --> 37:50.000
has two Pipe Majors, 

37:50.000 --> 37:54.000
and I’m going to ask both of those Pipe Majors some questions.

37:54.000 --> 37:56.000
So here’s the first one.

37:56.000 --> 37:58.000
What is your name?
Clare Gillies/Gillis

37:58.000 --> 38:04.000
Clare Gillies/Gillis. And why are you called the Pipe Major Clare?

38:04.000 --> 38:08.000
Well I took school examinations and I became one of the leaders of the band.

38:08.000 --> 38:10.000
Now, what is your name?

38:10.000 --> 38:12.000
Vera MacDonald

38:12.000 --> 38:14.000
You’re the second Pipe Major. 

38:14.000 --> 38:18.000
And could you tell me Vera, just how those pipes really work. 

38:18.000 --> 38:21.000
Does it take a lot of hard work to play them?

38:21.000 --> 38:23.000
No, the only part it takes is blowing them up. 

38:23.000 --> 38:26.000
And they consist of the chanter which contains the reed and the holes, 

38:26.000 --> 38:30.000
and there’s the bag into which the air goes, 

38:30.000 --> 38:34.000
and there’s three drones and in these three drones there are three reeds which carry the sound of the pipes.

38:34.000 --> 38:39.000
Now, the chanter is that sort of short pipe with the little holes, 

38:39.000 --> 38:47.000
and that is the place where your fingers do all that work as you are playing. 

38:47.000 --> 38:51.000
And each pipe has how many different … how many pipes coming from it. 

38:53.000 --> 38:59.000
I mean, how many pipes in the lot? Four? Four pipes and the bag.

38:59.000 --> 39:01.000
Yes.

39:01.000 --> 39:04.000
I know there’s ... the bag at the end of your pipes are different coloured 

39:04.000 --> 39:07.000
tartans too aren’t they?

39:08.000 --> 39:12.000
Now, you have some medals there on your chest, 

39:12.000 --> 39:14.000
or your doublet. 

39:14.000 --> 39:17.000
Clare, what are they for?

39:17.000 --> 39:20.000
I won them for dancing and piping at the Gaelic Mòd.

39:20.000 --> 39:26.000
You dance Highland dances, besides playing the pipes.

39:26.000 --> 39:30.000
Now, where has your band played besides in Cape Breton, Vera?

39:30.000 --> 39:35.000
We have played at New Glasgow, Prince Edward Island, Antigonish and Chicago, 

39:35.000 --> 39:37.000
at the Ninth International Convention.

39:37.000 --> 39:42.000
The Lions Club of Sydney and New Waterford sponsored that trip.

39:42.000 --> 39:43.000
Yes.

39:43.000 --> 39:44.000
Did you have a nice time there?

39:44.000 --> 39:46.000
Yes, we had a wonderful time.

39:46.000 --> 39:49.000
Were you … were you on television when you were in Chicago?

39:49.000 --> 39:50.000
Yes.

39:50.000 --> 39:51.000
Did you enjoy that?

39:51.000 --> 39:52.000
Did we ever…?

39:52.000 --> 39:56.000
Well now the girls are going to play the “Hundred Pipers” for us. 

39:56.000 --> 39:58.000
That’s a lively piece.

40:42.000 --> 40:46.000
Now I thought you might like to talk to the youngest girl in the band, 

40:46.000 --> 40:48.000
and she’s a bonnie one indeed with blonde curls. 

40:48.000 --> 40:50.000
And what is your name?

40:50.000 --> 40:52.000
Bunnie MacLeod
Bonnie MacLeod?
Bunnie.

40:52.000 --> 40:55.000
Bunnie. Well it might be either one. 

40:55.000 --> 40:57.000
Now, how old are you?

40:57.000 --> 40:58.000
Nine.

40:58.000 --> 41:00.000
How long have you been playing these pipes?

41:00.000 --> 41:03.000
I don’t play the pipes. I play the drums.

41:03.000 --> 41:05.000
Oh, you play the drums. 

41:05.000 --> 41:09.000
Now that drum is pretty large for a little girl like you. 

41:09.000 --> 41:13.000
And how long have you been playing?

41:13.000 --> 41:14.000
Two years.

41:14.000 --> 41:16.000
Two years. You began when you were about seven.

41:16.000 --> 41:17.000
Yes.

41:17.000 --> 41:19.000
Now, did you go to Chicago too?

41:19.000 --> 41:20.000
Yes.

41:20.000 --> 41:22.000
And how long were you there?

41:22.000 --> 41:23.000
Ten days.

41:23.000 --> 41:24.000
Ten days. 

41:24.000 --> 41:25.000
Were you lonesome while you were there?

41:25.000 --> 41:27.000
No.

41:27.000 --> 41:28.000
You’d like to go back.

41:28.000 --> 41:29.000
Yes.

41:29.000 --> 41:31.000 
Well I’m sure you will some day, 

41:31.000 --> 41:33.000
and I’m sure they’d be glad to have you back there.

41:33.000 --> 41:35.000
Were you on television too?

41:35.000 --> 41:37.000
Yes.

41:37.000 --> 41:41.000
And now the girls are going to play “The 42nd Highlanders” for you. 

41:41.000 --> 41:43.000
I’m sure you would like to hear that drum 

41:43.000 --> 41:47.000
the youngest girl in the band was telling us she played.

42:35.000 --> 42:40.000
And now we’re going to end with “The Road to the Isles”

44:41.000 --> 44:45.000
Well how did you enjoy the skirl of the pipes this morning? 

44:45.000 --> 44:50.000
I’m sure they helped you wash your morning dishes a little faster. 

44:50.000 --> 44:55.000
This is Alison Grant saying goodbye until tomorrow morning.